  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Mannitol, a small polar polyol, has human absolute oral bioavailability of 0.228 at 150 g and about 20% at 100 g (C01, C06, C09, C17); L3 exposure records show dose-linear Cmax and AUC after 50–150 g oral mannitol (C35–C38, C40–C43, C45–C46), consistent with meaningful oral absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C06", "C09", "C17", "C35", "C36", "C37", "C38", "C40", "C41", "C42", "C43", "C45", "C46"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Glucose, another small polar sugar, had oral bioavailability estimates of 0.722 in healthy subjects (C16), 0.843 in type 1 diabetic patients (C10), and 38% in hypoglycemic children (C15), all at or above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C10", "C15", "C16"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Sorbitol and propylene glycol, small polyols, showed oral bioavailability of 0.36–0.38 in shunted patients (C03, C19) and very high/close-to-complete in adults (C13, C28, C29), supporting that small polar polyhydroxy compounds can meet F ≥ 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C03", "C19", "C13", "C28", "C29"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 Caco-2 records show mannitol has low apparent permeability (e.g., Papp ≤1×10^-6 cm/s; C48, C56, C57) and is used as a paracellular marker; because mannitol still reaches ~20% human F, low Caco-2 permeability alone is not affirmative evidence for F < 20% in a small polar molecule.",
      "card_ids": ["C48", "C56", "C57", "C01", "C06"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Gemcitabine's low oral bioavailability (10%, range 5–17%; rat solution 9.86%) is attributed to first-pass metabolism by cytidine deaminase and high first-pass clearance (C07, C18, C20, C21, C26, C30), a mechanism not applicable to ascorbic acid; these low outcomes do not transfer.",
      "card_ids": ["C07", "C18", "C20", "C21", "C26", "C30"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 records for uridine (C39) and clavulanic acid (C44) show oral exposure in mice/dogs but provide no absolute F or threshold-relevant comparison for ascorbic acid; L4 erythritol record (C50) indicates high water solubility but does not quantify F.",
      "card_ids": ["C39", "C44", "C50"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C47", "C48", "C49", "C50", "C51", "C52", "C53", "C54", "C55", "C56", "C57", "C58"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct ascorbic acid oral bioavailability or matched oral/IV AUC record is present in the supplied cards.",
    "No direct ascorbic acid Caco-2, intestinal permeability, or solubility measurement is present; L4 records are for mannitol and erythritol.",
    "Active transport (SVCT1/2) contribution to ascorbic acid absorption is inferred from the query prior, not from a measured record.",
    "Dose dependence and saturation of ascorbic acid absorption are not captured.",
    "Transfer from mannitol/glucose/sorbitol/propylene glycol contexts is qualitative; species, doses, and patient status differ."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F ≥ 20%): L1/L2 human F estimates for mannitol (~20%), glucose (0.72–0.84), sorbitol (0.36–0.38), and propylene glycol (very high) support that small polar polyhydroxy compounds can meet the threshold; L3 mannitol exposure records show dose-linear Cmax/AUC; L4 Caco-2 records show mannitol has low permeability yet still reaches ~20% human F, so low permeability alone is not affirmative low-F evidence. No direct ascorbic acid low-F record is present, and the query prior notes active transport as a possible enhancer. Prediction remains high with moderate confidence."
}

